WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.603 --> 00:00:07.520
ANTON CHUVAKIN: Hello,
and welcome to scale-up

00:00:07.520 --> 00:00:08.840
your security telemetry.

00:00:08.840 --> 00:00:11.440
This is Anton Chuvakin with
Google Cloud and Chronicle.

00:00:11.440 --> 00:00:13.190
I want to highlight
some of the challenges

00:00:13.190 --> 00:00:15.023
and some of the solutions
that organizations

00:00:15.023 --> 00:00:17.570
face in their battles
to optimize and improve

00:00:17.570 --> 00:00:20.870
their security operations
and improve their detection.

00:00:20.870 --> 00:00:22.520
I want to start
with trying to give

00:00:22.520 --> 00:00:26.990
a bit of a landscape around
threats, security operations,

00:00:26.990 --> 00:00:29.210
and highlight some of the
things that matter today.

00:00:29.210 --> 00:00:31.490
A lot of the things we
encounter in this space

00:00:31.490 --> 00:00:33.860
are artifacts of
the past errors.

00:00:33.860 --> 00:00:36.895
We have to deal with issues
that are inherited by companies

00:00:36.895 --> 00:00:39.253
and that have been
dealt with for years.

00:00:39.253 --> 00:00:40.670
However, there are
also new things

00:00:40.670 --> 00:00:43.250
that our predecessors
doing security operations

00:00:43.250 --> 00:00:44.670
have not seen.

00:00:44.670 --> 00:00:46.670
I also wanted to highlight
the role of the cloud

00:00:46.670 --> 00:00:50.300
as both something to monitor,
somewhere to detect threats

00:00:50.300 --> 00:00:54.330
in, and also an enabler for
modern security operations.

00:00:54.330 --> 00:00:56.450
And then next I wanted to
cover the details there

00:00:56.450 --> 00:00:58.310
on Chronicle, the modern
security analytics

00:00:58.310 --> 00:01:02.450
platform that gathers some of
the advantages of the cloud

00:01:02.450 --> 00:01:05.510
to enable you to detect threats
in both on premise and cloud

00:01:05.510 --> 00:01:06.410
environments.

00:01:06.410 --> 00:01:07.910
Afterwards, I want
to highlight some

00:01:07.910 --> 00:01:09.618
of the practical
recommendations that you

00:01:09.618 --> 00:01:13.550
can follow whether you are
a Chronicle customer or not.

00:01:13.550 --> 00:01:16.220
Now, why do so many
security presentations start

00:01:16.220 --> 00:01:17.630
from a threat landscape?

00:01:17.630 --> 00:01:19.370
To me, I do that
because I want to get

00:01:19.370 --> 00:01:20.750
on the same page with everybody.

00:01:20.750 --> 00:01:22.850
I wanted to highlight
some of the things

00:01:22.850 --> 00:01:26.000
we see with other organizations
and some of the challenges you

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:27.500
face today.

00:01:27.500 --> 00:01:30.740
Now, every security
presentation that I've seen,

00:01:30.740 --> 00:01:32.960
almost every one I've
seen in my entire career,

00:01:32.960 --> 00:01:35.180
starts from a
discussion of badness.

00:01:35.180 --> 00:01:38.270
Maybe hackers, intrusion,
breaches, crimeware.

00:01:38.270 --> 00:01:41.090
The words may change, but
the approach does not.

00:01:41.090 --> 00:01:42.740
Ultimately, the
security profession

00:01:42.740 --> 00:01:44.540
is driven by the threat.

00:01:44.540 --> 00:01:49.940
We are driven by what the bad
guy is trying to throw at us.

00:01:49.940 --> 00:01:52.610
In that sense, security is a
unique domain of knowledge.

00:01:52.610 --> 00:01:54.320
A lot of the things
we do in security

00:01:54.320 --> 00:01:56.570
are quite different
to what's done in IT

00:01:56.570 --> 00:01:59.690
because much of what
we do is about dealing

00:01:59.690 --> 00:02:03.410
with what an outsider, a
threat actor, or malware is

00:02:03.410 --> 00:02:05.010
about to throw this way.

00:02:05.010 --> 00:02:05.847
Why is it like this?

00:02:05.847 --> 00:02:07.430
What are some of the
things that would

00:02:07.430 --> 00:02:09.289
happen in the near future?

00:02:09.289 --> 00:02:10.842
Now, some of you
may think, hey, it's

00:02:10.842 --> 00:02:12.050
a painfully obvious question.

00:02:12.050 --> 00:02:13.400
This is a domain of security.

00:02:13.400 --> 00:02:17.400
We protect against hackers,
attackers, malware, all that.

00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:20.180
However, there is actually a
specific reason, in my opinion,

00:02:20.180 --> 00:02:22.790
as to why it's the case.

00:02:22.790 --> 00:02:27.350
Now, our world, unfortunately,
looks like this.

00:02:27.350 --> 00:02:30.080
This is my very
unscientific visual

00:02:30.080 --> 00:02:34.670
that shows changes in the number
of security analysts, security

00:02:34.670 --> 00:02:37.190
engineers, security
professionals in general,

00:02:37.190 --> 00:02:40.520
and the amount of stuff,
amounts of technology that

00:02:40.520 --> 00:02:41.930
needs securing.

00:02:41.930 --> 00:02:44.870
Think, those of you who started
your security career many years

00:02:44.870 --> 00:02:46.700
ago probably remember
the time when

00:02:46.700 --> 00:02:50.660
you had to secure servers
in a data center, maybe web

00:02:50.660 --> 00:02:54.060
servers, maybe file servers,
and essentially nothing else.

00:02:54.060 --> 00:02:55.603
This was, perhaps,
in the late '90s

00:02:55.603 --> 00:02:57.020
or maybe even
before that for some

00:02:57.020 --> 00:02:58.820
of the more enlightened
ones of you.

00:02:58.820 --> 00:03:02.450
Now, today we face a fairly
dizzying array of technologies

00:03:02.450 --> 00:03:06.980
to secure from, well, the same
servers, going all the way back

00:03:06.980 --> 00:03:10.010
to mainframes and technologies
that predate servers,

00:03:10.010 --> 00:03:12.440
going all the way back
to '80s or maybe '70s.

00:03:12.440 --> 00:03:14.400
But we also have
cloud computing,

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:16.340
mobility, we have
internet of things,

00:03:16.340 --> 00:03:18.230
we have all the
analytics pipelines.

00:03:18.230 --> 00:03:20.480
We have the emergent AI.

00:03:20.480 --> 00:03:23.690
We have a lot of technologies
within the domain

00:03:23.690 --> 00:03:28.820
of digital technology,
spanning IT and OT, Operational

00:03:28.820 --> 00:03:32.360
Technology, control systems,
and IoT, Internet of Things.

00:03:32.360 --> 00:03:34.130
In essence, what we have today--

00:03:34.130 --> 00:03:36.380
and we will expect to
continue having this--

00:03:36.380 --> 00:03:38.840
is that the number
of technologies,

00:03:38.840 --> 00:03:42.980
systems, things to
secure will keep growing.

00:03:42.980 --> 00:03:45.500
And most everybody
agrees that it

00:03:45.500 --> 00:03:49.220
would keep growing faster than
the number of security people.

00:03:49.220 --> 00:03:51.620
So many of the
challenges with breaches,

00:03:51.620 --> 00:03:57.170
badness, alert overload, missed
attacks stem from the fact

00:03:57.170 --> 00:03:59.670
that we just don't
have enough people.

00:03:59.670 --> 00:04:01.520
So what can possibly
be done to help it?

00:04:01.520 --> 00:04:03.560
You can automate things.

00:04:03.560 --> 00:04:04.920
But we'll touch on this.

00:04:04.920 --> 00:04:08.630
But the problem is that this
is the world we live in.

00:04:08.630 --> 00:04:11.240
And in this world, we do
have an increasing amount

00:04:11.240 --> 00:04:12.630
of things we have to secure.

00:04:12.630 --> 00:04:15.260
How do we ever hope
to win the battle?

00:04:15.260 --> 00:04:17.320
We'll talk about it.

00:04:17.320 --> 00:04:21.339
Now, in many presentations,
people talk about threats.

00:04:21.339 --> 00:04:24.430
And today I wanted to kind
of offer my own spin on this

00:04:24.430 --> 00:04:27.730
and say these are popular
security incident types.

00:04:27.730 --> 00:04:30.130
Now, admittedly,
in recent years,

00:04:30.130 --> 00:04:31.990
ransomware have
become a big thing.

00:04:31.990 --> 00:04:34.300
And it's been a
good number of years

00:04:34.300 --> 00:04:37.690
since it became the
scourge that it is now.

00:04:37.690 --> 00:04:41.680
Phishing attacks continue to be
a big part of modern security

00:04:41.680 --> 00:04:42.383
incidents.

00:04:42.383 --> 00:04:44.050
If you look at any
industry reports that

00:04:44.050 --> 00:04:48.010
cover items that were involved
in breaches and attacks,

00:04:48.010 --> 00:04:51.280
phishing will almost
always make an appearance.

00:04:51.280 --> 00:04:53.170
Now, the data
theft, whether it's

00:04:53.170 --> 00:04:57.580
payment data, valuable corporate
data, private citizens' data,

00:04:57.580 --> 00:04:58.960
is still there.

00:04:58.960 --> 00:05:00.820
While ransomware is
a type of malware,

00:05:00.820 --> 00:05:04.780
we still see a fair number of
other malware, web malware,

00:05:04.780 --> 00:05:07.420
traditional viruses and trojans.

00:05:07.420 --> 00:05:09.538
Service attacks continue
to be interesting.

00:05:09.538 --> 00:05:11.080
And this is the
types of attacks I've

00:05:11.080 --> 00:05:13.480
dealt with in my past
life as an analyst.

00:05:13.480 --> 00:05:14.860
The interesting
bit about this is

00:05:14.860 --> 00:05:18.220
that while some consider
it to be a nuisance,

00:05:18.220 --> 00:05:21.240
others consider it now a
service to their business

00:05:21.240 --> 00:05:22.230
[INAUDIBLE] attack.

00:05:22.230 --> 00:05:26.290
Now, to me, I hesitate
when I put insider threats

00:05:26.290 --> 00:05:29.380
on any kind of a top
popular list of attacks.

00:05:29.380 --> 00:05:31.810
I do see organizations
that are deeply concerned

00:05:31.810 --> 00:05:33.070
with insider threat.

00:05:33.070 --> 00:05:36.000
And these may be well in
the cloud or on premise.

00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:38.650
Insider threat continues
to be more of a low burning

00:05:38.650 --> 00:05:41.620
issue for some, but
there are organizations

00:05:41.620 --> 00:05:44.180
that are quite heavily
focused on insider threat.

00:05:44.180 --> 00:05:46.360
So I wanted to
highlight as number six

00:05:46.360 --> 00:05:49.810
my unauthorized access and
insider threat challenges.

00:05:49.810 --> 00:05:52.660
So to me this is
the list of things

00:05:52.660 --> 00:05:55.480
that keeps modern chief security
officers asleep at night

00:05:55.480 --> 00:05:57.310
or maybe nervous during the day.

00:05:57.310 --> 00:05:59.170
Now, there may be other
threats and issues

00:05:59.170 --> 00:06:02.110
that you deal with in your
business or in your industry,

00:06:02.110 --> 00:06:04.540
but these are kind of
top popular issues.

00:06:04.540 --> 00:06:06.080
And if you're doing
the monitoring,

00:06:06.080 --> 00:06:08.230
if you're doing security
operations planning,

00:06:08.230 --> 00:06:13.280
you're probably focusing
somewhere on these issues.

00:06:13.280 --> 00:06:16.150
Now, the spectrum of attacks
does go all the way up.

00:06:16.150 --> 00:06:19.810
And, of course, there are rare
but highly damaging attacks.

00:06:19.810 --> 00:06:23.920
All the so-called state
sponsored or just top tier

00:06:23.920 --> 00:06:25.720
cybercrime actors.

00:06:25.720 --> 00:06:31.180
There is a type of a threat
that it shows up in, well,

00:06:31.180 --> 00:06:34.900
in the news for sure sometimes,
in organizational security

00:06:34.900 --> 00:06:35.830
monitoring dashboards.

00:06:35.830 --> 00:06:39.770
But threats that,
for example, target

00:06:39.770 --> 00:06:42.980
Google, going back many
years ago, many of them

00:06:42.980 --> 00:06:44.240
are top tier threats.

00:06:44.240 --> 00:06:45.980
So while we talk
about popular threats,

00:06:45.980 --> 00:06:47.480
the reason I want
to show this slide

00:06:47.480 --> 00:06:52.947
is while you may fight
malware every day,

00:06:52.947 --> 00:06:55.280
it will probably be useful
to keep aware of the top tier

00:06:55.280 --> 00:06:56.888
threat actors that
are very much human

00:06:56.888 --> 00:06:58.430
and not automated--
and not automated

00:06:58.430 --> 00:07:02.530
systems that can be good
at computers and at people.

00:07:02.530 --> 00:07:05.200
In essence, when you
focus on popular threats,

00:07:05.200 --> 00:07:07.540
don't forget that there
is a type of a threat that

00:07:07.540 --> 00:07:09.850
may affect your
organizations that's very far

00:07:09.850 --> 00:07:12.310
out, or up, from that spectrum.

00:07:12.310 --> 00:07:14.800
This reminder will help
us to contextualize

00:07:14.800 --> 00:07:17.980
some of the security analytics
requirements we'll get to.

00:07:17.980 --> 00:07:20.620
Now, security operations
challenges specifically,

00:07:20.620 --> 00:07:23.410
challenges that SOCs
deal with, very often

00:07:23.410 --> 00:07:27.200
focus on these things I
highlighted in the slide.

00:07:27.200 --> 00:07:30.590
Now, if you recall the
slide with a visual,

00:07:30.590 --> 00:07:32.417
with my linear
graph, you do realize

00:07:32.417 --> 00:07:35.000
that keep acquiring and keeping
talented people to do security

00:07:35.000 --> 00:07:36.590
operations is challenging.

00:07:36.590 --> 00:07:39.260
Now, there are many other
things that come up.

00:07:39.260 --> 00:07:43.430
Obviously there's the growth
of spectrum, technologies,

00:07:43.430 --> 00:07:47.300
complexity of IT, mixture
of new and old IT,

00:07:47.300 --> 00:07:51.770
addition of OT and IoT, all puts
additional stress to security

00:07:51.770 --> 00:07:52.520
operations.

00:07:52.520 --> 00:07:54.470
Now, number five
on that slide is

00:07:54.470 --> 00:07:56.960
kind of self-inflicted,
because in many cases,

00:07:56.960 --> 00:08:01.310
in my experience, security teams
and security operations teams

00:08:01.310 --> 00:08:05.210
aren't really friends with
some IT operations teams.

00:08:05.210 --> 00:08:09.350
Now, at mature, enlightened
organizations, they are.

00:08:09.350 --> 00:08:12.550
But in some of the mainstream
organizations, they are not.

00:08:12.550 --> 00:08:14.450
Think patching, think
asset management,

00:08:14.450 --> 00:08:16.070
think DevOps challenges.

00:08:16.070 --> 00:08:19.850
Security in IT does have an
occasional friction point.

00:08:19.850 --> 00:08:22.040
To me these are
important to consider

00:08:22.040 --> 00:08:23.750
when you're planning
threat detection

00:08:23.750 --> 00:08:25.760
and monitoring those
security operations.

00:08:25.760 --> 00:08:29.275
Now, one thing to note is
that many of these challenges

00:08:29.275 --> 00:08:31.400
are similar, at least
philosophically, conceptually

00:08:31.400 --> 00:08:33.860
similar, to what we
had 5 or 10 years ago,

00:08:33.860 --> 00:08:35.720
but they're definitely bigger.

00:08:35.720 --> 00:08:37.363
There's a lot more IT.

00:08:37.363 --> 00:08:38.780
There's a lot more
security tools,

00:08:38.780 --> 00:08:40.710
because there are more alerts.

00:08:40.710 --> 00:08:43.117
And there is a lot
more of the opinion

00:08:43.117 --> 00:08:45.200
that we should deploy the
latest technology rather

00:08:45.200 --> 00:08:49.110
than to refine and
improve operations.

00:08:49.110 --> 00:08:52.260
I think that's enough of
the landscape, frankly.

00:08:52.260 --> 00:08:54.900
I think that I presented
a few slides here

00:08:54.900 --> 00:08:58.140
to kind of get on the same
page with the audience in terms

00:08:58.140 --> 00:09:00.310
of what an organization
may deal with.

00:09:00.310 --> 00:09:03.210
Now, cloud computing, by
the way, as I promised,

00:09:03.210 --> 00:09:06.150
highlights and shows
up as both something

00:09:06.150 --> 00:09:09.870
to monitor systems deployed
in the cloud, micro services,

00:09:09.870 --> 00:09:12.722
other cloud components,
but also as an enabler

00:09:12.722 --> 00:09:13.680
of security monitoring.

00:09:13.680 --> 00:09:15.660
That's where we
get to Chronicle.

00:09:15.660 --> 00:09:19.230
Now, you may not like
another challenges slide,

00:09:19.230 --> 00:09:21.510
but I wanted to present
this particular visual

00:09:21.510 --> 00:09:24.180
to kind of highlight the
comparison between how

00:09:24.180 --> 00:09:27.810
Chronicle Security Analytics
platform is built versus some

00:09:27.810 --> 00:09:30.180
of the existing tools.

00:09:30.180 --> 00:09:31.410
I can call them legacy.

00:09:31.410 --> 00:09:34.140
I would say in IT security, if
something is more than 10 years

00:09:34.140 --> 00:09:36.420
old, it's definitely legacy.

00:09:36.420 --> 00:09:39.280
The idea is some of
the technologies that

00:09:39.280 --> 00:09:43.140
are born before the cloud are a
little bit difficult to scale,

00:09:43.140 --> 00:09:45.720
or they may scale physically,
but they would not

00:09:45.720 --> 00:09:47.580
scale economically.

00:09:47.580 --> 00:09:49.200
And at the same time,
there are always

00:09:49.200 --> 00:09:54.550
challenges with telemetry,
types of data being incomplete.

00:09:54.550 --> 00:09:57.750
Now, when we are deploying
a security monitoring

00:09:57.750 --> 00:10:03.330
tool in 2020, we
would be seeing things

00:10:03.330 --> 00:10:07.210
that didn't exist 5
years ago, 10 years ago.

00:10:07.210 --> 00:10:09.510
And to me, this
matters because I

00:10:09.510 --> 00:10:12.250
want to focus on detecting
the popular threats,

00:10:12.250 --> 00:10:15.130
but also I want to focus on
detecting the modern threats.

00:10:15.130 --> 00:10:16.740
I want my security
analytics platform

00:10:16.740 --> 00:10:18.580
to grow with the threats.

00:10:18.580 --> 00:10:20.790
And I think that's
occasionally missing

00:10:20.790 --> 00:10:22.410
from the traditional tools.

00:10:22.410 --> 00:10:23.550
So enough of that.

00:10:23.550 --> 00:10:25.290
I want to go deeper
into Chronicle

00:10:25.290 --> 00:10:28.380
to highlight some of the
advantages of our approach.

00:10:28.380 --> 00:10:32.590
Now, many companies,
many organizations

00:10:32.590 --> 00:10:36.790
deploy with a mission statement
or with a high level vision.

00:10:36.790 --> 00:10:38.440
Many of them are
somewhat generic.

00:10:38.440 --> 00:10:42.130
I wanted to briefly
point at the Chronicle

00:10:42.130 --> 00:10:45.490
vision of building a
planet-scale system for storing

00:10:45.490 --> 00:10:47.260
and analyzing
enterprise telemetry

00:10:47.260 --> 00:10:49.870
and making it useful for
detection hunting and response.

00:10:49.870 --> 00:10:51.430
Now, this to me is very crisp.

00:10:51.430 --> 00:10:54.430
And to me, when I joined
Chronicle and then ultimately

00:10:54.430 --> 00:10:57.910
Google Cloud, that was a big
part of that [INAUDIBLE]..

00:10:57.910 --> 00:10:59.950
Because to me, as
an analyst, I wanted

00:10:59.950 --> 00:11:03.520
to look at things that scale,
that scale economically,

00:11:03.520 --> 00:11:07.840
that cover modern use cases,
can cover traditional use cases,

00:11:07.840 --> 00:11:12.190
and can also cover many of the
security operations challenges.

00:11:12.190 --> 00:11:16.070
Detecting, nowadays threat
hunting, responding to issues.

00:11:16.070 --> 00:11:19.240
So to me this is
a way to do that.

00:11:19.240 --> 00:11:22.120
Chronicle to me is a way
to build a modern security

00:11:22.120 --> 00:11:24.100
analytics platform
rather than just

00:11:24.100 --> 00:11:27.040
to try evolving a SIEM slowly.

00:11:27.040 --> 00:11:27.730
That matters.

00:11:27.730 --> 00:11:29.230
Some of you in the
audience may have

00:11:29.230 --> 00:11:31.750
deployed security
information event management

00:11:31.750 --> 00:11:34.250
tools, or SIEMs, for a
good number of years.

00:11:34.250 --> 00:11:37.750
And there's a long history of
fairly challenging deployment

00:11:37.750 --> 00:11:40.390
scenarios as we learned
from the previous slide.

00:11:40.390 --> 00:11:41.440
I wanted to build--

00:11:41.440 --> 00:11:44.800
we wanted to build a platform
that's easy, it's deployable,

00:11:44.800 --> 00:11:48.160
and it does leverage
unique Google assets

00:11:48.160 --> 00:11:50.690
like threat intelligence
and, of course, search speed.

00:11:50.690 --> 00:11:53.400
No debate here.

00:11:53.400 --> 00:11:56.080
Now, I want to briefly
attract your attention

00:11:56.080 --> 00:11:58.720
to the architecture
of the platform.

00:11:58.720 --> 00:12:01.950
Now, those of you who have seen
security information and event

00:12:01.950 --> 00:12:04.320
management tools,
log management tools,

00:12:04.320 --> 00:12:06.930
may recognize some
of the components.

00:12:06.930 --> 00:12:10.140
I wanted to point at some
things that are different.

00:12:10.140 --> 00:12:15.090
Now, for the SIEM tools
that are built in the '90s

00:12:15.090 --> 00:12:18.420
or in the early 2000s,
threat detection

00:12:18.420 --> 00:12:20.190
and instant investigation
and, of course,

00:12:20.190 --> 00:12:22.200
a few other use cases
that are essential,

00:12:22.200 --> 00:12:25.110
threat hunting,
however, was not.

00:12:25.110 --> 00:12:27.900
Chronicle is one of the few
SIEMs built with threat hunting

00:12:27.900 --> 00:12:30.455
as a first-class use
case for a platform.

00:12:30.455 --> 00:12:31.830
It's kind of a
group of use cases

00:12:31.830 --> 00:12:33.760
rather, not just one use case.

00:12:33.760 --> 00:12:36.150
So to me, the ability to
search and rapidly pivot

00:12:36.150 --> 00:12:39.450
and refine signals while
you're doing, while you're

00:12:39.450 --> 00:12:43.950
pursuing a pretty subtle or
a thin indicator of an attack

00:12:43.950 --> 00:12:44.680
is important.

00:12:44.680 --> 00:12:47.240
And I don't want to just
rely on search or on reports

00:12:47.240 --> 00:12:49.630
or on rule-based
detection for doing that.

00:12:49.630 --> 00:12:52.500
I want my interactive
and pivoting search

00:12:52.500 --> 00:12:55.410
platform that's instant.

00:12:55.410 --> 00:12:57.920
There are a few other things
that are interesting here.

00:12:57.920 --> 00:13:01.460
Chronicle makes certain types
of telemetry like DNS and DHCP

00:13:01.460 --> 00:13:05.160
logs to also be a
first-class data source.

00:13:05.160 --> 00:13:07.550
Now, I've dealt with
SIEMs for-- at this point,

00:13:07.550 --> 00:13:10.280
for almost 20 years-- for
18 years, to be exact.

00:13:10.280 --> 00:13:15.160
And at this point, I've
seen people not put DNS, not

00:13:15.160 --> 00:13:18.260
put DHCP logs, not put
file hashes in their SIEMs

00:13:18.260 --> 00:13:20.240
because they are
just overwhelmed.

00:13:20.240 --> 00:13:23.132
In case of Chronicle, you
would want to put this data in,

00:13:23.132 --> 00:13:24.590
and it would not
cost you anything.

00:13:24.590 --> 00:13:27.380
I'll get to the costing
point in a second.

00:13:27.380 --> 00:13:29.350
Of course, today's
security operations

00:13:29.350 --> 00:13:31.510
is unthinkable
without heavy reliance

00:13:31.510 --> 00:13:33.420
on threat intelligence.

00:13:33.420 --> 00:13:35.033
In case of Chronicle,
you would have,

00:13:35.033 --> 00:13:36.450
if you have your
own threat intel,

00:13:36.450 --> 00:13:38.000
you have public sources--

00:13:38.000 --> 00:13:39.975
some of them are more
noisier than others--

00:13:39.975 --> 00:13:42.100
all their threat intel data
flows into the platform

00:13:42.100 --> 00:13:44.560
without any challenges, and
it would cross-correlated

00:13:44.560 --> 00:13:47.030
to logs in a few ways.

00:13:47.030 --> 00:13:50.200
I'll explain that
in the next slide.

00:13:50.200 --> 00:13:53.110
Now, this slide is probably
more useful to people

00:13:53.110 --> 00:13:56.980
who have dealt with threat
detection, security monitoring,

00:13:56.980 --> 00:14:00.880
threat investigation challenges,
and/or deployed similar tools.

00:14:00.880 --> 00:14:03.770
I wanted to hit a
few points here,

00:14:03.770 --> 00:14:06.040
some in more detail than others.

00:14:06.040 --> 00:14:09.640
First, even though
I'm a technologist,

00:14:09.640 --> 00:14:12.100
I want to start with
disruptive economics.

00:14:12.100 --> 00:14:14.550
Now, what does disruptive
economics really mean?

00:14:14.550 --> 00:14:17.890
Today, it means a pricing model
for a security monitoring tool

00:14:17.890 --> 00:14:19.240
that's different.

00:14:19.240 --> 00:14:21.460
Now, some of you are
used to seeing tools

00:14:21.460 --> 00:14:25.720
that deploy and price based
on data volume collected,

00:14:25.720 --> 00:14:29.080
or the number of data sources,
the number of log sources,

00:14:29.080 --> 00:14:31.630
or on the number of
components deployed.

00:14:31.630 --> 00:14:33.770
There is a lot of complexity.

00:14:33.770 --> 00:14:36.800
And of course, there is also
a charge for data retention

00:14:36.800 --> 00:14:39.140
if you're storing data
for a year, for example.

00:14:39.140 --> 00:14:43.150
So Chronicle launched with a
pricing model that runs off

00:14:43.150 --> 00:14:45.860
the number of employees.

00:14:45.860 --> 00:14:49.090
People, not data sources,
not data points, not events

00:14:49.090 --> 00:14:50.120
per second.

00:14:50.120 --> 00:14:53.470
Now, this means that you have
fully predictable pricing.

00:14:53.470 --> 00:14:56.420
And predictable just means,
if you're a large company,

00:14:56.420 --> 00:14:58.900
you count your employees,
and that's the price.

00:14:58.900 --> 00:15:01.810
Now, this is
incredibly liberating,

00:15:01.810 --> 00:15:04.590
let me tell you that,
for a security solution.

00:15:04.590 --> 00:15:06.340
The reason for that
is that a lot of tools

00:15:06.340 --> 00:15:09.190
are prized by data volume
or some derivative of that.

00:15:09.190 --> 00:15:11.470
And that means you have
to balance your security

00:15:11.470 --> 00:15:15.280
requirements with the fear that
you would overrun the license,

00:15:15.280 --> 00:15:17.300
you'd incur costs.

00:15:17.300 --> 00:15:19.690
And in case of
Chronicle, you kind of

00:15:19.690 --> 00:15:22.330
pay a fixed price per year.

00:15:22.330 --> 00:15:24.790
And by the way, that
fixed price includes

00:15:24.790 --> 00:15:26.680
one year of log retention.

00:15:26.680 --> 00:15:31.000
Not 30 days, not 90 days,
not 180 days, a full year.

00:15:31.000 --> 00:15:32.470
And you can pay
the same, and you'd

00:15:32.470 --> 00:15:34.420
get data retained
for more than that.

00:15:34.420 --> 00:15:37.190
To me this is also
an important point.

00:15:37.190 --> 00:15:39.160
Now, keeping data for
a year isn't that hard.

00:15:39.160 --> 00:15:41.890
It's expensive for some other
people, but it's not hard.

00:15:41.890 --> 00:15:44.140
Now, how about searching
across petabytes

00:15:44.140 --> 00:15:45.880
of data on subsecond speed?

00:15:45.880 --> 00:15:49.030
I'm going to move into the
bottom item on the slide.

00:15:49.030 --> 00:15:50.890
Admittedly, very
few people at Google

00:15:50.890 --> 00:15:53.830
are impressed by search speed
because, hey, Google can search

00:15:53.830 --> 00:15:56.480
internet at the same speed.

00:15:56.480 --> 00:16:00.160
Now, in the security operations
threat detection space,

00:16:00.160 --> 00:16:02.290
instantaneous searches
over petabytes

00:16:02.290 --> 00:16:06.730
of data just aren't that common,
or in fact, if they do happen

00:16:06.730 --> 00:16:09.640
to somebody, it's usually at
the cost of hardware measured

00:16:09.640 --> 00:16:12.400
in millions, if not
tens of millions.

00:16:12.400 --> 00:16:14.170
Chronicle is built
in a way that it

00:16:14.170 --> 00:16:17.440
can search structured
data in under a second

00:16:17.440 --> 00:16:19.150
over any volume of data.

00:16:19.150 --> 00:16:20.200
And we really mean that.

00:16:20.200 --> 00:16:21.670
To me, when I
first learned that,

00:16:21.670 --> 00:16:24.208
it was a huge, huge innovation.

00:16:24.208 --> 00:16:26.125
Even though, again, the
team that presented it

00:16:26.125 --> 00:16:28.510
to me, the Chronicle
team at the time,

00:16:28.510 --> 00:16:30.400
didn't really act as
if it's a big deal.

00:16:30.400 --> 00:16:31.870
Of course, they're from Google.

00:16:31.870 --> 00:16:34.900
Now, to me, being able to
search a petabyte of data

00:16:34.900 --> 00:16:39.040
at subsecond speed does enable
a lot of investigative hunting

00:16:39.040 --> 00:16:42.760
and detection use cases
in a very clean way.

00:16:42.760 --> 00:16:44.650
Let me hit a few more
points on this slide

00:16:44.650 --> 00:16:48.610
and then I'm going to go
into details on two of them.

00:16:48.610 --> 00:16:51.570
We also notice that
in the past with tools

00:16:51.570 --> 00:16:53.860
that collect the data
but don't make it useful,

00:16:53.860 --> 00:16:56.490
the quality of data inside a
security monitoring platform

00:16:56.490 --> 00:16:57.660
is a big challenge.

00:16:57.660 --> 00:17:00.210
We decided to focus
very heavily on having

00:17:00.210 --> 00:17:03.900
clean, enriched cross-correlated
data on our platform.

00:17:03.900 --> 00:17:06.869
Here on the slide is highlighted
as intelligent data fusion.

00:17:06.869 --> 00:17:08.760
That means we can
collect the data

00:17:08.760 --> 00:17:11.940
and we can make it useful for
security operations use cases

00:17:11.940 --> 00:17:13.980
by connecting
different data pieces.

00:17:13.980 --> 00:17:15.990
Firewall data
connected to EDR data,

00:17:15.990 --> 00:17:18.597
connected to server data,
connected to other data

00:17:18.597 --> 00:17:20.430
sources you collect,
and, of course, the DNS

00:17:20.430 --> 00:17:23.470
and DHCP as I highlighted.

00:17:23.470 --> 00:17:26.560
We can also use this enriched
data to cross-correlate

00:17:26.560 --> 00:17:27.760
with threat intelligence.

00:17:27.760 --> 00:17:30.710
And they can do that both
historically and in real-time.

00:17:30.710 --> 00:17:35.180
I'm going to make another point
about this in the next slide.

00:17:35.180 --> 00:17:38.300
Also, we decided to
build a detection engine

00:17:38.300 --> 00:17:41.900
into this platform that can
detect threats that are modern,

00:17:41.900 --> 00:17:42.920
that appear today.

00:17:42.920 --> 00:17:46.370
Of course, we would cover the
popular threats on that slide,

00:17:46.370 --> 00:17:48.200
but we also want
to be future proof.

00:17:48.200 --> 00:17:50.000
So if new types
of threats appear,

00:17:50.000 --> 00:17:52.310
we would be able to
write detection content

00:17:52.310 --> 00:17:53.297
to catch them.

00:17:53.297 --> 00:17:55.130
Of course, self-managed
is a no brainer here

00:17:55.130 --> 00:17:57.230
because it comes
with the cloud model.

00:17:57.230 --> 00:17:59.610
Chronicle, after all,
is a SaaS platform.

00:17:59.610 --> 00:18:01.910
So in that sense,
we don't suffer

00:18:01.910 --> 00:18:04.490
from some of the complexities
of on premise SIEMs.

00:18:04.490 --> 00:18:07.110
That's an obvious point.

00:18:07.110 --> 00:18:08.470
How do customers use it?

00:18:08.470 --> 00:18:10.060
I want to highlight
this very briefly.

00:18:10.060 --> 00:18:13.360
Because, in many
regards, we are deployed

00:18:13.360 --> 00:18:15.860
to extend another solution.

00:18:15.860 --> 00:18:18.340
You may have a tool that you
use for compliance reporting,

00:18:18.340 --> 00:18:21.340
for certain types of
workflow, but you're not

00:18:21.340 --> 00:18:23.050
handling the volume well.

00:18:23.050 --> 00:18:25.780
We do see Chronicle
deployed with other tools

00:18:25.780 --> 00:18:27.130
to enhance this.

00:18:27.130 --> 00:18:28.750
Of course, we also
see situations

00:18:28.750 --> 00:18:30.880
where Chronicle is
deployed as their SIEM,

00:18:30.880 --> 00:18:32.980
as the main security
information event management

00:18:32.980 --> 00:18:36.280
tool for their SOC, for their
security operations team.

00:18:36.280 --> 00:18:39.550
And in this case, they
migrate from a historical SIEM

00:18:39.550 --> 00:18:41.530
to our platform.

00:18:41.530 --> 00:18:43.580
In the middle, you
see another scenario.

00:18:43.580 --> 00:18:47.650
In this case, we do see
customers deploy Chronicle to

00:18:47.650 --> 00:18:49.420
on top of EDR.

00:18:49.420 --> 00:18:51.340
There's some popular
EDR solutions

00:18:51.340 --> 00:18:53.670
that produce telemetry
that's very useful for threat

00:18:53.670 --> 00:18:56.200
detection and investigation,
but they don't actually

00:18:56.200 --> 00:18:57.610
retain it for a year.

00:18:57.610 --> 00:18:59.890
Now, you can refer to
some of the writing

00:18:59.890 --> 00:19:02.140
we have done on the
Chronicle website

00:19:02.140 --> 00:19:04.340
about how this is valuable.

00:19:04.340 --> 00:19:06.880
I want to highlight the fact
that if you are investigating,

00:19:06.880 --> 00:19:08.297
there's nothing
better than having

00:19:08.297 --> 00:19:12.590
super quick access to one year
of data, or more than that.

00:19:12.590 --> 00:19:16.570
Now, one thing I mentioned
briefly is detection.

00:19:16.570 --> 00:19:18.670
And one of the big
surprises for me

00:19:18.670 --> 00:19:20.860
when I first encountered
Chronicle technology

00:19:20.860 --> 00:19:24.520
was that Chronicle relied a
lot on threat intelligence

00:19:24.520 --> 00:19:28.480
and rules rather than, say,
machine learning as some

00:19:28.480 --> 00:19:29.470
of the vendors.

00:19:29.470 --> 00:19:31.600
Now, we do think that
machine learning is

00:19:31.600 --> 00:19:33.050
a good approach for detection.

00:19:33.050 --> 00:19:36.010
However, we also think that for
machine learning to work well,

00:19:36.010 --> 00:19:38.590
you have to learn from
something, from real world

00:19:38.590 --> 00:19:42.130
experiences of hunters,
incident responders, and not

00:19:42.130 --> 00:19:43.840
just from the vendor labs.

00:19:43.840 --> 00:19:46.420
So we have deployed Chronicle
with the rule-based detection,

00:19:46.420 --> 00:19:49.180
with intel-based detection
that covers petabytes.

00:19:49.180 --> 00:19:52.720
Can run on very high
quality enriched data

00:19:52.720 --> 00:19:55.390
that we have so we can
match to enrich data,

00:19:55.390 --> 00:19:59.290
say, IP addresses or DNS names,
even if threat indicators don't

00:19:59.290 --> 00:20:00.730
match verbatim.

00:20:00.730 --> 00:20:05.080
We also have a way to write
detection logic that's

00:20:05.080 --> 00:20:09.820
not about writing queries
but about writing detection.

00:20:09.820 --> 00:20:14.820
Writing detections here means
writing focused use cases

00:20:14.820 --> 00:20:17.940
to detect threats, not just
extract data and then think

00:20:17.940 --> 00:20:19.110
what it means.

00:20:19.110 --> 00:20:20.910
This to me is kind
of a big deal.

00:20:20.910 --> 00:20:23.010
[INAUDIBLE] is our
approach to this.

00:20:23.010 --> 00:20:25.142
And you can see the
white paper on the site.

00:20:25.142 --> 00:20:27.100
There's a lot of content
written on [INAUDIBLE]

00:20:27.100 --> 00:20:28.230
and how it's evolved.

00:20:31.030 --> 00:20:33.250
I did want to very
briefly remind you

00:20:33.250 --> 00:20:37.470
about the productivity that
stems from subsecond responses

00:20:37.470 --> 00:20:38.290
of search.

00:20:38.290 --> 00:20:40.090
Again, my first encounter
with the platform

00:20:40.090 --> 00:20:43.210
highlighted that if you
have tens of gigabytes

00:20:43.210 --> 00:20:45.290
of data, hundreds of
gigs or petabytes,

00:20:45.290 --> 00:20:46.780
the search speed
would be the same.

00:20:46.780 --> 00:20:48.580
And that does allow
it to run queries,

00:20:48.580 --> 00:20:52.190
run detection logic that is
not constrained by performance.

00:20:52.190 --> 00:20:53.890
This to me is a very big point.

00:20:53.890 --> 00:20:57.280
I don't want my SOC to be in
the performance optimization

00:20:57.280 --> 00:20:57.880
business.

00:20:57.880 --> 00:21:00.520
I want them to be in security
business, threat detection

00:21:00.520 --> 00:21:04.330
business, not in, oh, my God,
my SIEM is too slow business.

00:21:04.330 --> 00:21:07.300
I want them to run
detection logic instantly,

00:21:07.300 --> 00:21:10.230
and Chronicle does enable that.

00:21:10.230 --> 00:21:12.870
Now, as a technologist,
I am probably

00:21:12.870 --> 00:21:16.050
more inclined to talk
about features, advantages,

00:21:16.050 --> 00:21:19.590
capabilities, detection logic,
but for Chronicle, disruptive

00:21:19.590 --> 00:21:21.780
economics is kind of a
big part of the attraction

00:21:21.780 --> 00:21:22.770
of the platform.

00:21:22.770 --> 00:21:25.920
To me, nobody was able to build
a software service monitoring

00:21:25.920 --> 00:21:29.130
platform, a threat detection
platform that does not rely

00:21:29.130 --> 00:21:31.872
on any volume pricing on data.

00:21:31.872 --> 00:21:33.830
Admittedly, some of the
advantages you see here

00:21:33.830 --> 00:21:35.750
are inherent with
any SaaS tools.

00:21:35.750 --> 00:21:40.010
No infrastructure setup costs
and quite rapid searches.

00:21:40.010 --> 00:21:44.120
But things like all the data you
want collected at a fixed cost

00:21:44.120 --> 00:21:44.990
is unique.

00:21:44.990 --> 00:21:48.140
And to me, this eliminates
many of the challenges

00:21:48.140 --> 00:21:49.670
that modern SOCs face.

00:21:49.670 --> 00:21:50.990
You want to have more data?

00:21:50.990 --> 00:21:51.950
Have more data.

00:21:51.950 --> 00:21:54.260
That's a decision that
should be based on the merits

00:21:54.260 --> 00:21:56.180
and not on the costs.

00:21:56.180 --> 00:21:58.910
This to me is what's changing
the landscape of some

00:21:58.910 --> 00:22:01.700
of the SOC operations that
I've encountered, where

00:22:01.700 --> 00:22:03.900
you can include the
data sources you want,

00:22:03.900 --> 00:22:09.980
EDR, deeper service telemetry,
some of the cloud data sources.

00:22:09.980 --> 00:22:11.960
And not pay anything
for any extra sources

00:22:11.960 --> 00:22:14.940
is hugely liberating.

00:22:14.940 --> 00:22:17.940
Now, if you're planning
the deployment of our tool,

00:22:17.940 --> 00:22:21.250
of Chronicle, it's useful
to highlight a few items

00:22:21.250 --> 00:22:22.400
when you're doing this.

00:22:22.400 --> 00:22:26.350
Now, we do have tools to support
the total cost of ownership

00:22:26.350 --> 00:22:27.310
analysis.

00:22:27.310 --> 00:22:28.990
Admittedly, most
vendors do that.

00:22:28.990 --> 00:22:32.620
But what you would see in our
case is that many of the TCO

00:22:32.620 --> 00:22:35.290
calculations, many
of the TCO arguments,

00:22:35.290 --> 00:22:38.310
because they derive their magic
from the predictable price

00:22:38.310 --> 00:22:41.920
and decoupled from capacity,
compute, number of things

00:22:41.920 --> 00:22:45.820
you run, so to me this is a
very big part of an attraction,

00:22:45.820 --> 00:22:47.580
as I pointed out.

00:22:47.580 --> 00:22:49.710
And again, some of the
things you see on this slide

00:22:49.710 --> 00:22:52.110
are characteristics
of all modern tools

00:22:52.110 --> 00:22:54.420
like, for example,
[INAUDIBLE] and APIs.

00:22:54.420 --> 00:22:57.340
But some things on that slide
are very uniquely Google.

00:22:57.340 --> 00:23:00.060
Of course, we rely on some
of the threat intel that

00:23:00.060 --> 00:23:03.690
stems from Google's research
and Google's teams that

00:23:03.690 --> 00:23:05.670
focus on identifying
threat actors,

00:23:05.670 --> 00:23:08.670
but we also highlight
things like a rapid delivery

00:23:08.670 --> 00:23:11.040
model, rapid deployment,
and, of course,

00:23:11.040 --> 00:23:14.220
subsecond searches over
any source of data.

00:23:14.220 --> 00:23:16.230
To me, many of the
clients and prospects

00:23:16.230 --> 00:23:17.700
who are looking
at Chronicle have

00:23:17.700 --> 00:23:20.300
evaluated things similar
to those in the slide

00:23:20.300 --> 00:23:21.800
and decided to go
with the platform.

00:23:24.590 --> 00:23:26.740
Let's get to the final
part of the presentation,

00:23:26.740 --> 00:23:30.660
namely, the action items.

00:23:30.660 --> 00:23:32.970
I would hypothesize that
many of the listeners

00:23:32.970 --> 00:23:35.580
have some form of security
information event management

00:23:35.580 --> 00:23:38.370
tool or a log management
tool, whether open source,

00:23:38.370 --> 00:23:41.670
commercial, whether software
as a service or on-premise.

00:23:41.670 --> 00:23:45.510
Now, one thing to note is
that if your approach today is

00:23:45.510 --> 00:23:48.740
heavily on-premise based,
I think the time has

00:23:48.740 --> 00:23:50.370
come to evaluate it.

00:23:50.370 --> 00:23:52.940
Now, I would not pretend that
Chronicle is the only tool.

00:23:52.940 --> 00:23:57.870
There is maybe a set of half a
dozen approaches, half a dozen

00:23:57.870 --> 00:24:02.240
tools, vendors that started to
build software as a service,

00:24:02.240 --> 00:24:05.070
SIEM, software as a service
threat detection tools.

00:24:05.070 --> 00:24:07.260
Back in [INAUDIBLE] days,
I've looked at them,

00:24:07.260 --> 00:24:09.600
and I found some of them
promising, some of them

00:24:09.600 --> 00:24:10.530
not promising.

00:24:10.530 --> 00:24:12.950
The idea is that the
on-premise approach starts

00:24:12.950 --> 00:24:15.120
to slowly lose the importance.

00:24:15.120 --> 00:24:17.970
And I would say, if I'm
planning my threat detection

00:24:17.970 --> 00:24:20.220
platform of the future,
it would probably

00:24:20.220 --> 00:24:21.450
be software as a service.

00:24:21.450 --> 00:24:23.700
It will not be a legacy tool
deployed in [INAUDIBLE]..

00:24:23.700 --> 00:24:25.860
It would not be a legacy
tool deployed on-premise.

00:24:25.860 --> 00:24:28.410
It would be a native SaaS tool.

00:24:28.410 --> 00:24:31.800
Advantages and the future
proofness of such tool,

00:24:31.800 --> 00:24:35.700
if I may call it, coin a
term here, I just unmatch.

00:24:35.700 --> 00:24:39.520
Now, you may think that
deploying SaaS-based, software

00:24:39.520 --> 00:24:43.830
as a service-based tools, makes
sense for cloud environments.

00:24:43.830 --> 00:24:45.050
And you would be right.

00:24:45.050 --> 00:24:47.610
However, my point
is that it's also

00:24:47.610 --> 00:24:50.080
useful for on-premise
environments.

00:24:50.080 --> 00:24:53.490
Some of the advantages of SaaS,
lack of maintenance management

00:24:53.490 --> 00:24:57.010
performance tuning, much
faster content updates,

00:24:57.010 --> 00:24:59.580
much more rapid deployment
of detection rule,

00:24:59.580 --> 00:25:03.060
normalization, other
types of advantages

00:25:03.060 --> 00:25:05.000
are there and then here in SaaS.

00:25:05.000 --> 00:25:08.222
In case of Chronicle, they are
coupled with super rapid speed,

00:25:08.222 --> 00:25:10.180
backwards and forward to
indicate our matching,

00:25:10.180 --> 00:25:13.042
and, of course, our unique
threat detection language.

00:25:13.042 --> 00:25:15.000
Now, I did mention that
Chronicle is a software

00:25:15.000 --> 00:25:16.350
as a service platform.

00:25:16.350 --> 00:25:19.110
And I mentioned some of the
advantages of SaaS platforms

00:25:19.110 --> 00:25:21.090
for monitoring and
threat detection.

00:25:21.090 --> 00:25:23.910
Now, there would be
still a large group

00:25:23.910 --> 00:25:26.550
of organizations that
would prefer some help even

00:25:26.550 --> 00:25:27.330
with SaaS.

00:25:27.330 --> 00:25:29.130
Now, as I said,
with SaaS you don't

00:25:29.130 --> 00:25:32.520
need to operate the
servers, performance tune,

00:25:32.520 --> 00:25:34.920
but you still need to
do things associated

00:25:34.920 --> 00:25:37.790
with the typical security
operations workflow,

00:25:37.790 --> 00:25:39.870
respond to alerts,
respond to incidents,

00:25:39.870 --> 00:25:41.730
perform threat hunting.

00:25:41.730 --> 00:25:43.740
What's useful to know
is that Chronicle

00:25:43.740 --> 00:25:46.710
enables a decent number of
managed security providers,

00:25:46.710 --> 00:25:49.940
or MDR providers, for
managed detection response.

00:25:49.940 --> 00:25:53.620
And those providers
do take the last mile.

00:25:53.620 --> 00:25:54.880
They help you with alerts.

00:25:54.880 --> 00:25:57.623
They help you with defining
what type of detection to do.

00:25:57.623 --> 00:25:59.040
They help you with
figure out what

00:25:59.040 --> 00:26:02.910
threats you need to add to the
list of the popular threats,

00:26:02.910 --> 00:26:05.380
which threats are
focused on your business.

00:26:05.380 --> 00:26:08.670
So to me, the value of Chronicle
for managed security providers

00:26:08.670 --> 00:26:12.005
is also huge because of
the cost model, the cost

00:26:12.005 --> 00:26:13.440
of the detection content.

00:26:13.440 --> 00:26:15.210
So to me, the useful
thing to keep in mind

00:26:15.210 --> 00:26:17.490
is that if your
company thinks it's not

00:26:17.490 --> 00:26:19.170
ready for deploying this--

00:26:19.170 --> 00:26:21.750
I personally doubt it-- but
if you are in that bucket,

00:26:21.750 --> 00:26:24.870
you can find a managed security
services provider, an MDR

00:26:24.870 --> 00:26:26.405
provider, that runs Chronicle.

00:26:26.405 --> 00:26:29.220
So you'd gain many of the
advantages of the platform,

00:26:29.220 --> 00:26:32.520
but you'd also get help
with writing your SOC,

00:26:32.520 --> 00:26:35.500
writing your
security operations.

00:26:35.500 --> 00:26:37.560
Now, to conclude, I wanted
to highlight the fact

00:26:37.560 --> 00:26:40.380
that Chronicle remains
a growing platform.

00:26:40.380 --> 00:26:42.310
The product is very young.

00:26:42.310 --> 00:26:44.730
So there would be things
that we will have to build.

00:26:44.730 --> 00:26:46.840
We are still building
the workflow system.

00:26:46.840 --> 00:26:49.440
We are still building a few
other things around detection.

00:26:49.440 --> 00:26:51.840
And, of course, we want to
make our detection smarter

00:26:51.840 --> 00:26:54.090
by using machine learning
and AI techniques.

00:26:54.090 --> 00:26:58.600
The idea here is that we have
unique and strong advantages

00:26:58.600 --> 00:27:00.220
in the platform
that I highlighted,

00:27:00.220 --> 00:27:03.520
but we also remain to be a work
in progress to a large extent

00:27:03.520 --> 00:27:05.590
as every good
product is, frankly.

00:27:05.590 --> 00:27:07.750
So to me, what we
invite you to do

00:27:07.750 --> 00:27:09.730
is not just to make
use of Chronicle today,

00:27:09.730 --> 00:27:11.530
but to also grow with us.

00:27:11.530 --> 00:27:14.130
Thank you very
much for listening.

